// An operator inside a method of a generic type. Marked xfail: any operator
// application in a generic struct's method body is rejected with
//
//   Method or field `+` not found.
//
// reported at the method and again per instantiation. The operands are
// irrelevant -- '1i64 + 2i64', with no name in it at all, fails exactly as
// 'count + 1i64' does -- so it is not about the type argument, the fields or
// the receiver.
//
// This became reachable only when generic methods started working at all;
// before that no method of a generic type was ever type checked, so nothing
// downstream of the declaration had run. generic-success covers what does
// work: a value receiver, a reference receiver returning the type argument,
// and a mutable one whose write reaches the caller.
//
// Measured, at the failing lookup: the receiver the operator is dispatched on
// is a VarNameUse of 'self' whose type is the instance, rather than the
// operand that was written -- which is the shape fnCallTypeCheck's
// "rewrite to self.method" branch produces when it folds a bare method name
// into the call it already had. The same operator in a generic *function*
// works, and in a non-generic struct's method works, so what to look at is
// what that branch and the cloner do to a method body between them.
//
// When this scenario starts passing, drop the xfail and fold it into
// generic-success beside methodsOnAGenericType.

struct Counter[T] {
  item T
  count i64

  fn bump(self &mut) {
    count = count + 1i64
  }
}

fn useIt() i64 {
  mut c = Counter[i64][item: 1i64, count: 0i64]
  (&mut c).bump()
  c.count
}
